  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased gender sensitive social protection and social security coverage including for those in the informal economy and vulnerable groups</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The United Republic of Tanzania is advancing its commitment to social protection as a key driver of poverty reduction and socioeconomic development, in alignment with the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Despite progress, a comprehensive social protection system that ensures coverage for all, including vulnerable groups such as women, persons with disabilities, and those in the informal economy, remains a challenge.
This CPO supports the Government in strengthening its legal and institutional frameworks for social protection, ensuring gender-sensitive and inclusive policies. Key interventions include enhancing the capacity of stakeholders such as government officials, workers&apos; organizations, and employers&apos; associations to design, implement, and monitor social protection systems. Utilizing tools like the TRANSFORM curriculum, these efforts aim to build long-term capacity for managing social protection floors.
A significant aspect of this CPO is support for the enactment of the Tanzania National Universal Health Insurance Act. This fundamental legislation seeks to ensure universal access to healthcare services for all citizens through a comprehensive health insurance system, strengthening the country&#x2019;s health financing mechanisms and reducing barriers to essential health services. Additionally, the ILO collaborates with the Government to adopt a National Social Protection Policy and develop mechanisms for sustainable financing, alongside technical support to improve the delivery of the PSSN program.
These measures are expected to extend social protection coverage, particularly to underserved groups, and contribute to poverty eradication, decent work, universal healthcare, and gender equality. This initiative is implemented in partnership with key constituents and aligns with broader UN efforts to promote inclusive, sustainable social protection systems in Tanzania</narrative>
  </description>
